			<div class="content" id="post-content-1653525">
									

<p>I agree with Fawkes on this one, it needs to be downgraded to Euclid. Unless I missed something, this thing technically passes the locked box test. Stick it in a giant, soundproof box, and don't open it, and nothing will happen that we know of yet. If you want to consider that not knowing what will happen, that still only makes it Euclid. Keter is defined rather well for us newbies as &quot;Stick it in a box and all hell breaks loose&quot;, which implies that an SCP requires constant maintenance of some sort in order to prevent a shitstorm; the main point of doing so being that we'd know if we did not perform X, Shitstorm Y would kill us all. We do not know yet if putting in a box and leaving it there for awhile will elicit a response. Euclid is defined as &quot;Stick it in a box not knowing what will happen&quot;, which fits this 'angel' rather well.</p>
<p>It's a very minor detail, but I'd think its classification in universe is just as important as anything else in the containment procedures. It sets the tone for the procedures themselves. That said, the rest is lovely. I raised an eyebrow at the neurotoxin part, but that could just be my opinion that a being of this nature wouldn't use pain-inducing means that we as humans are familiar with.</p>

